WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.930
In this video, you move the disk of a running virtual machine from Longhorn to Portworx with storage migration. The guest keeps running while its data moves.

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:19.740
The running virtual machine demo-smig boots from demo-smig-disk-0. Its storage class is a Longhorn class, made for the image.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:24.070
First, create the target volume. Select Create.

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:28.500
Use the same namespace as the virtual machine,

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.420
and name the volume demo-smig-px.

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:37.700
Choose the Portworx storage class px-csi-replicated.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:42.340
Set the size to ten gigabytes, the same as the source volume.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:44.830
Select Create.

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:49.680
The new volume is ready and not attached to any virtual machine.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.030
Now open the Virtual Machines page.

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:56.570
Open the actions menu for demo-smig,

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:59.780
and select Storage Migration.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:04.540
For the source volume, choose demo-smig-disk-0.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:13.510
For the target volume, choose demo-smig-px. The list shows only volumes that no virtual machine uses.

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.660
Select Apply.

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:26.340
SUSE Virtualization live-migrates the virtual machine to another node and copies the disk to the new volume.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:33.080
The migration is complete. The virtual machine kept running and now runs on a different node.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:35.900
Check the Volumes page.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:43.280
demo-smig-px, on the Portworx storage class, is now the disk of demo-smig.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:49.596
The virtual machine now runs from a Portworx volume and never stopped. Next, you hot-plug a CD-ROM.
